replace("docs/PROGRESS.md",
  "- Part 28-4a done (ADR 0056): the PIN form of POST /api/login ({display_name, pin, remember} -> {operator_id, display_name, session_id, session_token, expires_ts}; the token form is unchanged), POST /api/logout ({logged_out}; the thirteenth endpoint in s03-8.md), and the Port methods pin_login, start_login_session and logout (blocking, run in a worker thread). HostPort writes the failure count, lock, session row and revocation through Ingest.write as durable units committed before the answer. api/pin_auth.py is now planners shared by the host and the CLI. LOGIN_LOCKED once per lock, LOGOUT, LOGIN_OK and LOGIN_REJECTED with reason pin. LoginReq (models.py) accepts exactly one form. 20 new tests (test_api_login_pin.py, test_host_port_pin.py); test_api_endpoint_table.py expects 13 endpoints. Also fixes six tests that P28-2 left expecting two migrations (test_startup.py: REAL replaces the hard-coded [1, 2], and two_migrations numbers its test migration after the real ones; test_cli_backup.py: seed-roster expects [1, 2, 3]). No setting, CLI or dashboard change yet (P28-4b, P28-5).\n- Part 28-4b done (ADR 0056): `cli set-pin --operator-id N [--length 6..12 | --prompt]` (random PIN shown once, or typed twice and hidden; scrypt hash only; clears the lock and failure count, ends the operator's login sessions, writes PIN_SET offline); rotate-token and disable-operator revoke the operator's login sessions; the session_hours setting (default 12, range above 0 to 168, validated at load, in HASH_EXCLUDED) is read by HostPort for each new session. New tests: test_cli_pin.py, test_session_hours.py and one in test_host_port_pin.py; the key counts in test_settings.py and test_gen_constants.py go from 36 to 37 (HASH_EXCLUDED 17 to 18). No dashboard change yet (P28-5).\n",
  "- Part 28-4a done (ADR 0056): the PIN form of POST /api/login ({display_name, pin, remember} -> {operator_id, display_name, session_id, session_token, expires_ts}; the token form is unchanged), POST /api/logout ({logged_out}; the thirteenth endpoint in s03-8.md), and the Port methods pin_login, start_login_session and logout (blocking, run in a worker thread). HostPort writes the failure count, lock, session row and revocation through Ingest.write as durable units committed before the answer. api/pin_auth.py is now planners shared by the host and the CLI. LOGIN_LOCKED once per lock, LOGOUT, LOGIN_OK and LOGIN_REJECTED with reason pin. LoginReq (models.py) accepts exactly one form. 20 new tests (test_api_login_pin.py, test_host_port_pin.py); test_api_endpoint_table.py expects 13 endpoints. Also fixes six tests that P28-2 left expecting two migrations (test_startup.py: REAL replaces the hard-coded [1, 2], and two_migrations numbers its test migration after the real ones; test_cli_backup.py: seed-roster expects [1, 2, 3]). No setting, CLI or dashboard change yet (P28-4b, P28-5).\n- Part 28-4b done (ADR 0056): `cli set-pin --operator-id N [--length 6..12 | --prompt]` (random PIN shown once, or typed twice and hidden; scrypt hash only; clears the lock and failure count, ends the operator's login sessions, writes PIN_SET offline); rotate-token and disable-operator revoke the operator's login sessions; the session_hours setting (default 12, range above 0 to 168, validated at load, in HASH_EXCLUDED) is read by HostPort for each new session. New tests: test_cli_pin.py, test_session_hours.py and one in test_host_port_pin.py; the key counts in test_settings.py and test_gen_constants.py go from 36 to 37 (HASH_EXCLUDED 17 to 18). No dashboard change yet (P28-5).\n- Part 28-5 done (ADR 0056): the dashboard login. The Login page opens on name and PIN (6 to 12 digits, password field, emptied after each attempt) with \"Remember me on this browser\" off by default and an \"admin token\" link to the old form; a PIN login stores the host's session token in the same slot as the operator token, in sessionStorage or, with the box ticked, localStorage (store/token.ts reads it for REST, /ws and the worker). Log out in the top strip (asks once) calls POST /api/logout, clears both storages and shows Login, also when the host is down. Close code 4401 and a 401 on any logged-in REST call (api/authfetch.ts) clear both storages and show Login. New tests: login_pin.test.ts (api, controller, storage, 401 hook) and login_ui.test.tsx (screen, remember, lock text, Log out); shell.test.tsx and watchdog_ui.test.tsx follow the new default screen. The Playwright helper opens the admin token form first. No host change. P28-6 (Playwright PIN test with a mock-host PIN login, doctor row, close-out) not done.\n");
replace("docs/adr/0056.md",
  "- **Risk.** On a shared station, \"remember me\" means whoever uses that browser acts as that operator, and audit_events names that operator. Mitigations: off by default, 12 hours, Log out at shift change, and a `cli doctor` row for operators without a PIN (P28-6). Expired rows are kept for the audit trail; a purge is left open.\n- **Parts.** P28-1 docs and this ADR. P28-2 Migration 0003 (operators gains pin_hash, pin_salt, pin_fails, pin_locked_until, pin_set_ts; table login_sessions; three audit actions). P28-3 PIN and session logic in api/security.py. P28-4 endpoints and the set-pin CLI. P28-5 dashboard login screen, storage choice, Log out. P28-6 Playwright test, doctor row, close-out.\n- **Progress.** P28-1, P28-2, P28-3 and P28-4a done (Migration 0003; PIN and session logic; the PIN form of POST /api/login and POST /api/logout through the host port). P28-4b (CLI set-pin, the session_hours setting, revoke on rotate and disable) is done too; P28-5 and P28-6 are not.\n- **P28-3 notes.** The write side is host/ppe_host/api/pin_auth.py (set_pin, pin_login, create_session, revoke_session, revoke_operator_sessions): plain functions on a writable connection, no audit rows (the P28-4 route writes them through the port). Hashing, the PIN rule and the session check are in api/security.py. The scrypt check runs outside the write transaction. The lock resets pin_fails to 0 when it starts; a wrong PIN of a disabled operator or of an operator without a PIN is not counted. The session_hours setting is added in P28-4 with the wiring; until then security.DEFAULT_SESSION_HOURS (12) is the value, and Authenticator takes an optional now_ms clock.\n- **Checked here.** Nothing is run: this part changes Markdown only.",
  "- **Risk.** On a shared station, \"remember me\" means whoever uses that browser acts as that operator, and audit_events names that operator. Mitigations: off by default, 12 hours, Log out at shift change, and a `cli doctor` row for operators without a PIN (P28-6). Expired rows are kept for the audit trail; a purge is left open.\n- **Parts.** P28-1 docs and this ADR. P28-2 Migration 0003 (operators gains pin_hash, pin_salt, pin_fails, pin_locked_until, pin_set_ts; table login_sessions; three audit actions). P28-3 PIN and session logic in api/security.py. P28-4 endpoints and the set-pin CLI. P28-5 dashboard login screen, storage choice, Log out. P28-6 Playwright test, doctor row, close-out.\n- **Progress.** P28-1, P28-2, P28-3 and P28-4a done (Migration 0003; PIN and session logic; the PIN form of POST /api/login and POST /api/logout through the host port). P28-4b (CLI set-pin, the session_hours setting, revoke on rotate and disable) and P28-5 (the dashboard login screen, the storage choice, Log out) are done too; P28-6 is not.\n- **P28-3 notes.** The write side is host/ppe_host/api/pin_auth.py (set_pin, pin_login, create_session, revoke_session, revoke_operator_sessions): plain functions on a writable connection, no audit rows (the P28-4 route writes them through the port). Hashing, the PIN rule and the session check are in api/security.py. The scrypt check runs outside the write transaction. The lock resets pin_fails to 0 when it starts; a wrong PIN of a disabled operator or of an operator without a PIN is not counted. The session_hours setting is added in P28-4 with the wiring; until then security.DEFAULT_SESSION_HOURS (12) is the value, and Authenticator takes an optional now_ms clock.\n- **Checked here.** Nothing is run: this part changes Markdown only.");
replace("docs/adr/0056.md",
  "- **P28-4a notes.** POST /api/login takes {token} or {display_name, pin, remember} (exactly one form; a mix or a missing field is 422 and never echoes the PIN). The PIN form answers {operator_id, display_name, session_id, session_token, expires_ts}; the token form is unchanged. POST /api/logout is the thirteenth listed endpoint and answers {logged_out}. The Port gains pin_login, start_login_session and logout; they block (scrypt, the commit wait), so the routes call them in a worker thread, and HostPort sends the planned statements through Ingest.write as durable units that are committed before the answer: a failure count or a lock survives a restart, and a session is readable by the next request. api/pin_auth.py is now a set of planners (plan_login, plan_set_pin, plan_session, revoke_stmt) plus the same conn functions as before, so the host and the offline CLI share one decision. The failure count and lock are one atomic UPDATE. A locked operator answers 401 at once with retry_after_s = seconds left and no growing delay (no scrypt is run); every other failure is the same 401 'invalid name or PIN' with the global delay. LOGIN_LOCKED is written once, on the failure that starts the lock; LOGIN_REJECTED and LOGIN_OK carry reason pin. HostPort builds its Authenticator with the host clock, so a session expires by the same clock that created it. Session length is still security.DEFAULT_SESSION_HOURS (12): the setting is P28-4b.\n- **P28-4b notes.** `cli set-pin --operator-id N` gives the operator a random PIN (--length 6 to 12 digits, 6 by default) that is printed once; --prompt reads a typed PIN twice with getpass instead. A PIN is never an argument, so it is not in a shell history. Only the scrypt hash and salt are stored. It clears the failure count and the 15-minute lock (this is how an administrator unlocks an operator before the lock runs out), ends that operator's login sessions, refuses a disabled operator, and writes PIN_SET offline (session_id NULL, event_seq 0). rotate-token and disable-operator now also revoke the operator's login sessions in the same transaction (rotate-token prints how many ended, before the TOKEN line). The setting session_hours (default 12, above 0 and at most 168, checked when the settings load) sets the session length; it is in HASH_EXCLUDED, so changing it does not end a shift. It lives in host/config/settings.json and s01-settings.md; the design docx is not touched. HostPort reads it for every new session; sessions already made keep their own expiry.\n",
  "- **P28-4a notes.** POST /api/login takes {token} or {display_name, pin, remember} (exactly one form; a mix or a missing field is 422 and never echoes the PIN). The PIN form answers {operator_id, display_name, session_id, session_token, expires_ts}; the token form is unchanged. POST /api/logout is the thirteenth listed endpoint and answers {logged_out}. The Port gains pin_login, start_login_session and logout; they block (scrypt, the commit wait), so the routes call them in a worker thread, and HostPort sends the planned statements through Ingest.write as durable units that are committed before the answer: a failure count or a lock survives a restart, and a session is readable by the next request. api/pin_auth.py is now a set of planners (plan_login, plan_set_pin, plan_session, revoke_stmt) plus the same conn functions as before, so the host and the offline CLI share one decision. The failure count and lock are one atomic UPDATE. A locked operator answers 401 at once with retry_after_s = seconds left and no growing delay (no scrypt is run); every other failure is the same 401 'invalid name or PIN' with the global delay. LOGIN_LOCKED is written once, on the failure that starts the lock; LOGIN_REJECTED and LOGIN_OK carry reason pin. HostPort builds its Authenticator with the host clock, so a session expires by the same clock that created it. Session length is still security.DEFAULT_SESSION_HOURS (12): the setting is P28-4b.\n- **P28-4b notes.** `cli set-pin --operator-id N` gives the operator a random PIN (--length 6 to 12 digits, 6 by default) that is printed once; --prompt reads a typed PIN twice with getpass instead. A PIN is never an argument, so it is not in a shell history. Only the scrypt hash and salt are stored. It clears the failure count and the 15-minute lock (this is how an administrator unlocks an operator before the lock runs out), ends that operator's login sessions, refuses a disabled operator, and writes PIN_SET offline (session_id NULL, event_seq 0). rotate-token and disable-operator now also revoke the operator's login sessions in the same transaction (rotate-token prints how many ended, before the TOKEN line). The setting session_hours (default 12, above 0 and at most 168, checked when the settings load) sets the session length; it is in HASH_EXCLUDED, so changing it does not end a shift. It lives in host/config/settings.json and s01-settings.md; the design docx is not touched. HostPort reads it for every new session; sessions already made keep their own expiry.\n- **P28-5 notes.** The dashboard Login page opens on name and PIN (the PIN field is a password field and is emptied after every attempt; Sign in stays off until there is a name and 6 to 12 digits), with a \"Remember me on this browser\" box that is off by default and a link \"Use the admin token instead\" to the old token form (and back). The host's session token is kept in the same slot as the operator token (`ppe_token`), in sessionStorage, or in localStorage when the box is ticked (if the browser blocks localStorage the token stays in the tab). Everything that sends a token (REST, /ws, the Web Worker's /ws/tick) reads that one slot through `store/token.ts`, so none of it changed. A 401 with retry_after_s of 60 s or more is the lock and says so in minutes and points to the admin token. Log out (top strip, asks once because the screen stops monitoring) calls POST /api/logout with the stored token, best effort, then clears both storages, stops the sockets and shows Login, also when the host is down. Close code 4401 now clears both storages, and a 401 on any logged-in REST call (`api/authfetch.ts`) does the same, so a session that ends in an open tab shows Login on the next call instead of a silent failure. The Playwright helper opens the admin token form first; a PIN end-to-end test with a mock host PIN login is P28-6.\n");
